---
import type { Tool } from '../data/tools';
import { type Locale } from '../data/locales';
import { toolName, getBlogKeywordsContent } from '../data/translations';

type Props = {
  tool: Tool;
  locale: Locale;
};

const { tool, locale } = Astro.props;
const useCases = tool.useCases.join(', ');
const controls = tool.controls.join(', ');
const name = toolName(locale, tool);
const blog = (tool.id === 'white-screen' || tool.id === 'black-screen' || tool.id === 'green-screen' || tool.id === 'blue-screen' || tool.id === 'red-screen' || tool.id === 'orange-screen' || tool.id === 'yellow-screen' || tool.id === 'purple-screen' || tool.id === 'pink-screen' || tool.id === 'cyan-screen' || tool.id === 'brown-screen' || tool.id === 'grey-screen' || tool.id === 'custom-color-screen' || tool.id === 'gradient-screen' || tool.id === 'rgb-screen' || tool.id === 'hex-screen' || tool.id === 'fireplace-screen' || tool.id === 'aquarium-screen' || tool.id === 'matrix-screen' || tool.id === 'snow-screen' || tool.id === 'ambient-screen' || tool.id === 'rain-screen' || tool.id === 'clock-screen' || tool.id === 'broken-screen' || tool.id === 'blue-screen-prank' || tool.id === 'windows-xp-crash' || tool.id === 'radar-screen' || tool.id === 'white-noise-screen' || tool.id === 'fake-virus-screen' || tool.id === 'windows-update' || tool.id === 'ubuntu-screen' || tool.id === 'macos-screen' || tool.id === 'android-update') ? getBlogKeywordsContent(locale, tool.id) : null;
---

<article class="site-shell rich-article max-w-3xl py-16">
  <h2>How to use {name} well.</h2>
  <p>
    {name} is designed for people who need a fast, dependable fullscreen utility instead of a noisy page wrapped around a tiny demo.
    The goal is {tool.intent}. That matters because screen tools are usually opened in the middle of another workflow: checking a new
    monitor, lighting a quick shot, preparing a classroom display, testing color output, staging a video scene, or creating a calm ambient
    background on a spare device.
  </p>
  <p>
    The page starts with the screen itself, then keeps the controls close enough to be useful without making them the product. Open fullscreen
    mode when you want the most accurate view. Browser tabs, bookmark bars, and desktop UI can hide edge defects, change perceived contrast,
    or make a color feel less even than it really is. Fullscreen mode gives the entire panel to the utility, and the Escape hint keeps the
    interaction understandable for keyboard and assistive-technology users.
  </p>
  <p>
    Common use cases for {name.toLowerCase()} include {useCases}. Each use case benefits from a clean page because surrounding graphics
    influence perception. A white border can make grey look darker. A colorful header can change how saturated a solid color appears. A heavy
    animation can distract from pixel-level inspection. This page keeps the interface quiet and lets the screen state take priority.
  </p>
  <h2>Controls and workflow.</h2>
  <p>
    The reusable screen component includes {controls}. These controls are intentionally consistent across the site, so a person who learns one
    screen can move through color screens, testing tools, ambient screens, fake screens, and prank screens without relearning the interface.
    Designers can copy values, testers can adjust brightness, creators can enter an exact color, and casual users can simply press Fullscreen.
  </p>
  <p>
    Brightness should be changed gradually. Displays do not all respond the same way, and a phone, OLED television, office monitor, and projector
    can produce very different perceived output at the same nominal brightness. If you are checking for panel defects, raise brightness enough
    to reveal the issue, then lower it again to a comfortable level. If you are using the screen as light, test the scene through the camera or
    the viewer that matters rather than relying on how the panel looks from your chair.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keyboard shortcuts make repeat use faster. Press F for fullscreen, R for a random color where available, and C to copy the current color.
    The controls are standard form controls wherever possible, which keeps the page accessible and predictable. The page also respects reduced
    motion preferences for motion-heavy effects, because a screen saver or prank page should not become uncomfortable for users who prefer a
    calmer interface.
  </p>
  {tool.kind === 'saver' && tool.id === 'fireplace-screen' ? (
    <>
      <h2>Ambient setup and room atmosphere.</h2>
      <p>
        When using {name.toLowerCase()} for room decoration, focus sessions, evening relaxation, or holiday warmth, position your display at eye level or mount your Smart TV on a wall to emulate a natural hearth fireplace. Lowering room overhead lights enhances the ambient orange and red glow projected by the display onto surrounding surfaces.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fine-tune the crackling fire audio volume to complement your environment. Pair it with soft background lo-fi music, cozy lighting, a warm beverage, or quiet reading to build the ultimate soothing atmosphere in any home, apartment, office, coffee shop, studio, or waiting room.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : tool.kind === 'saver' && tool.id === 'clock-screen' ? (
    <>
      <h2>Focus setup and productivity workflow.</h2>
      <p>
        When using {name.toLowerCase()} for focus sessions, study sprints, or as a desktop companion, place it on a second monitor or beside your primary display. The flip clock provides an always-visible time reference without demanding attention, while the integrated Pomodoro timer helps structure your work into focused intervals and restorative breaks. The gentle flip animation and optional click sound create a subtle productivity rhythm.
      </p>
      <p>
        For optimal use, set the clock to 24-hour format or toggle seconds off depending on your preference. Switch to Countdown mode and select a Pomodoro interval that matches your workflow. Adjust brightness downward in low-light environments to reduce eye strain and make the clock a comfortable presence rather than a distraction. The retro flip animation is designed to be satisfying without being intrusive, making it suitable for long-running sessions in offices, libraries, study rooms, and home workspaces.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : tool.kind === 'saver' ? (
    <>
      <h2>Ambient setup and room atmosphere.</h2>
      <p>
        When using {name.toLowerCase()} for room decoration, focus sessions, or evening relaxation, position your display at eye level or mount your Smart TV on a wall. Lowering room overhead lights enhances the ambient glow projected by the display onto surrounding surfaces for a more immersive visual experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fine-tune the ambient audio volume to complement your environment. Pair it with soft background lo-fi music, cozy lighting, a warm beverage, or quiet reading to build the ultimate soothing atmosphere in any home, apartment, office, coffee shop, studio, or waiting room.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : tool.kind === 'prank' && tool.id === 'radar-screen' ? (
    <>
      <h2>Radar display setup and immersion tips.</h2>
      <p>
        When using the <strong>radar screen simulator</strong> as a display background, presentation visual, or streaming overlay, fullscreen mode is
        essential for the most authentic effect. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">F</kbd> or click the fullscreen
        button to hide all browser UI and let the radar fill the entire display. The rotating sweep beam, concentric range rings, and compass headings
        create a convincing tactical dashboard aesthetic that works beautifully in both light and dark room environments.
      </p>
      <p>
        Customize the radar before or during use through the settings panel (gear icon). Adjust the sweep speed between 4 and 30 RPM — slower speeds
        produce a calm, realistic radar feel while faster speeds create tension and urgency. The three color themes let you match the display to your
        setting: Military Green for classic radar authenticity, Oceanic Blue for a modern naval sonar look, or Red Alert for high-stakes tactical
        scenarios. Enable geolocation to display real coordinates on the HUD, adding an extra layer of authenticity.
      </p>
      <p>
        The sonar ping audio adds significant immersion. Leave it on for a realistic command-center atmosphere, or mute it (press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">S</kbd>) when
        the radar is used as a quiet ambient background. For presentations, we recommend keeping the ping volume moderate so it complements without
        overpowering your voice. The radar animation continues even when paused or muted, making it a versatile and reliable visual tool for any
        setting — from gaming room decoration to professional livestream environments.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : tool.kind === 'prank' && tool.id === 'fake-virus-screen' ? (
    <>
      <h2>Fake virus screen setup and prank performance tips.</h2>
      <p>
        When using the <strong>fake virus screen simulator</strong> for a prank, video production, or theatrical effect, fullscreen mode is
        essential for the most convincing experience. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">F</kbd> or click the
        fullscreen button to hide all browser UI and let the fake security alert fill the entire display. The alarm sounds start automatically
        in fullscreen mode for complete immersion — each alert style has a unique synthesized audio signature created with the Web Audio API.
      </p>
      <p>
        Choose from 4 alert styles using the settings panel (gear icon) or keyboard shortcuts. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">1</kbd> for the Google Chrome malware warning ("Deceptive site ahead") — a realistic red warning page that mimics Chrome's safe browsing alert. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">2</kbd> for Microsoft Defender SmartScreen — a convincing site-blocked page with shield icon and expandable details. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">3</kbd> for the Windows Security antivirus dashboard — a full antivirus UI with fake scan progress, threat card, and a toast notification. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">4</kbd> for the WannaCry ransomware screen — a dramatic ransom note with countdown timers and bitcoin address input. Each style includes interactive elements that respond to clicks.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>fake virus screen</strong> simulates a convincing system alert, not an actual system event. It generates CSS animations, browser-level alerts, and synthesized audio entirely in the browser — it does not install software, modify system settings, access personal files, or download anything to your device. The simulation resets instantly when you press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">Esc</kbd>, leaving no trace. For the most effective pranks, stage the device in fullscreen before your audience sees the screen, mute unexpected device sounds that could break the illusion, and consider which alert style best matches your scenario — the Chrome warning works well for browser-focused setups, the Defender page mimics corporate security, the antivirus dashboard looks like an actual infection scan, and the ransomware screen creates the most dramatic reaction. Use responsibly for friendly jokes, video content, educational demonstrations, and theatrical productions.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : tool.kind === 'fake' && tool.id === 'windows-update' ? (
    <>
      <h2>Fake Windows Update screen setup and prank tips.</h2>
      <p>
        When using the <strong>fake Windows Update screen simulator</strong> for a prank, video production, or theatrical effect, fullscreen mode is
        essential for the most convincing experience. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">F</kbd> or click the
        fullscreen button to hide all browser UI and let the fake update screen fill the entire display. The simulation continues running
        smoothly in fullscreen mode with all animated elements active for maximum realism.
      </p>
      <p>
        Choose from 4 simulation modes using the settings panel (gear icon) or keyboard shortcuts. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">1</kbd> for Spinning Dots mode — the classic "Getting Windows ready" animation with circling dots and a "Don't turn off your computer" message. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">2</kbd> for Update Progress mode — a percentage progress bar with "Working on updates" text that simulates an active system update. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">3</kbd> for Blue Screen mode — a convincing Windows blue screen of death with sad face, error code, and "Collecting error info" progress bar. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">4</kbd> for Recovery mode — an "Automatic Repair" or "Preparing Automatic Repair" screen that looks like a system trying to diagnose itself after a failed boot.
      </p>
      <p>
        Adjust the speed slider in the settings panel to control how fast the animations play — slow for extended pranks and background effects, medium for a natural update feel, or fast for quick demos. Toggle "Show percentage" to display or hide the numerical progress. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">R</kbd> to pause and resume the animation at any time, creating the illusion of a stuck update. Each mode responds to these controls independently, giving you precise control over the simulation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>fake Windows Update screen</strong> simulates a convincing system event, not an actual system modification. It generates CSS animations and visual effects entirely in the browser — it does not install software, modify system settings, access personal files, or download anything to your device. The simulation resets instantly when you press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">Esc</kbd>, leaving no trace. For the most effective results at a prank, choose Blue Screen mode for maximum shock value, Spinning Dots for a believable update-in-progress scenario, or Recovery mode to suggest a system failure. Match the mode to your context for the most convincing effect.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : tool.kind === 'fake' && tool.id === 'ubuntu-screen' ? (
    <>
      <h2>Ubuntu screen simulator setup and Linux demo tips.</h2>
      <p>
        When using the <strong>Ubuntu screen simulator</strong> for a Linux demonstration, video production, or classroom presentation, fullscreen mode is
        essential for the most convincing experience. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">F</kbd> or click the
        fullscreen button to hide all browser UI and let the authentic Ubuntu-style update screen fill the entire display. The cursor auto-hides
        after 2 seconds of inactivity in fullscreen mode for a clean presentation look.
      </p>
      <p>
        The simulator starts automatically with a realistic Ubuntu boot screen featuring the Ubuntu logo and loading indicators. The progress
        simulation uses a non-linear curve that mimics genuine Ubuntu update behaviour — starting very slow, accelerating through the middle
        phases, then decelerating near completion. Random speed spurts of 0.5&ndash;5% jumps add authenticity, and the simulation pauses at
        common percentage points (5, 14, 28, 40, 55, 65, 76, 84, 92, 97) for 2&ndash;6 seconds at a time, just like real system updates do.
        A reboot cycle animation is built in, providing a complete update lifecycle visual.
      </p>
      <p>
        The visual design uses authentic Ubuntu brand colours — a dark purple background (<code class="rounded border border-hairline px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm">#300a24</code>) and Ubuntu orange accent
        (<code class="rounded border border-hairline px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm">#e95420</code>) — with the Ubuntu font family loaded for genuine appearance. Use the settings panel (gear icon) to adjust
        brightness and manage a timer, giving you full control over how the simulation appears. The Ubuntu terminal aesthetic makes this
        simulator ideal for any Linux-themed project, from classroom OS demonstrations to technology trade show displays.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>Ubuntu screen simulator</strong> simulates a convincing system update event, not an actual system modification. It generates CSS
        animations and visual effects entirely in the browser — it does not install software, modify system settings, access personal files,
        or download anything to your device. The simulation resets instantly when you press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">Esc</kbd>, leaving no trace. For the
        most effective demos, combine the Ubuntu simulator with our <a href="/fake-virus-screen/">fake virus screen</a> for a full
        system-prank scenario, or use the <a href="/windows-update/">Windows Update screen</a> alongside it for cross-platform comparison
        demonstrations.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : tool.kind === 'fake' && tool.id === 'macos-screen' ? (
    <>
      <h2>macOS update simulator setup and Apple demo tips.</h2>
      <p>
        When using the <strong>macOS update simulator</strong> for an Apple UI demonstration, video production, or classroom presentation, fullscreen mode is
        essential for the most convincing experience. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">F</kbd> or click the
        fullscreen button to hide all browser UI and let the authentic Apple-style update screen fill the entire display. The cursor auto-hides
        after 2 seconds of inactivity in fullscreen mode for a clean presentation look.
      </p>
      <p>
        The simulator starts automatically with a realistic macOS update screen featuring the Apple logo, update title (e.g. "macOS Sequoia 15.5"),
        status text ("Preparing...", "Downloading...", "Installing..."), and a progress bar. Choose from 5 update scenarios in the settings panel
        (gear icon): <strong>macOS Sequoia 15.5</strong> (7 phases: Preparing, Downloading, Verifying, Installing, Optimizing, Cleaning Up, Finishing),
        <strong>Security Response 7.2</strong> (6 phases), <strong>App Store Update</strong> (5 phases), <strong>BridgeOS Firmware Update</strong>
        (6 phases), or <strong>macOS Sequoia 16.0 Beta</strong> (7 phases). Each scenario has unique phase sequences and visual styling for a
        genuinely different look and feel.
      </p>
      <p>
        The progress simulation offers 4 operational modes. <strong>Realistic</strong> mode uses a non-linear curve with randomized speed variations,
        stick points at common percentages (10, 18, 30, 42, 55, 68, 78, 85, 92, 96, 98), and random pauses — just like a real macOS update.
        <strong>Timer</strong> mode completes linearly over a set duration (5, 10, 15, 30, or 60 minutes). <strong>Manual</strong> mode gives you
        full drag control. <strong>Infinite</strong> mode cycles near completion with periodic resets. Switch between 3 visual themes — macOS Dark,
        macOS Light, or Space Gray — and toggle Auto Restart for a two-phase restart animation or Sound for synthesized Apple-style chimes. The ETA
        display dynamically recalculates based on recent speed history for added realism.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>macOS update simulator</strong> simulates a convincing system update event, not an actual system modification. It generates CSS
        animations, phase transitions, and Web Audio API sounds entirely in the browser — it does not install software, modify system settings,
        access personal files, or download anything to your device. The simulation resets instantly when you press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">Esc</kbd>, leaving no trace.
        For the best cross-platform demos, combine this with the <a href="/windows-update/">Windows Update screen</a> and
        <a href="/ubuntu-screen/">Ubuntu screen simulator</a> for a complete operating system comparison presentation.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : tool.kind === 'fake' && tool.id === 'android-update' ? (
    <>
      <h2>Android update simulator setup and mobile UI demo tips.</h2>
      <p>
        When using the <strong>Android update simulator</strong> for a mobile UI demonstration, video production, or classroom presentation, fullscreen mode is
        essential for the most convincing experience. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">F</kbd> or click the
        fullscreen button to hide all browser UI and let the authentic Android-style update screen fill the entire display. The cursor auto-hides
        after 2 seconds of inactivity in fullscreen mode for a clean mobile presentation look.
      </p>
      <p>
        The simulator starts automatically with a realistic Android system update screen featuring the Android robot logo,
        "Installing system update" heading, and a progress bar. Open the settings panel (gear icon) to configure the update duration
        — choose from 1 minute, 2 minutes, 5 minutes (default), 10 minutes, 15 minutes, 30 minutes, or 1 hour. Adjust the
        <strong>starting position</strong> from 0% (fresh start) to 90% (nearly complete) to stage specific moments in the update
        cycle without waiting. The non-linear progress curve mimics real Android update behaviour with realistic speed variations.
      </p>
      <p>
        To trigger a convincing restart sequence, click the <strong>Restart</strong> button. Phase 1 shows "Restarting... Your device will restart shortly"
        with animated boot dots. Phase 2 shows "Starting Android..." with additional boot dots — simulating a complete device reboot cycle.
        This two-phase animation is perfect for demonstrating how Android devices behave during a system update restart, and it adds
        production value to any video or stream. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">Esc</kbd> at any time
        to exit fullscreen and instantly end the simulation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>Android update simulator</strong> simulates a convincing mobile system update event, not an actual system modification. It generates
        CSS animations and visual effects entirely in the browser — it does not install software, modify system settings, access personal files,
        or download anything to your device. The simulation resets instantly when you press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">Esc</kbd>, leaving no trace.
        For the best cross-platform mobile demos, combine this with our <a href="/windows-update/">Windows Update screen</a>,
        <a href="/ubuntu-screen/">Ubuntu screen simulator</a>, and <a href="/macos-screen/">macOS update simulator</a> for a complete
        operating system comparison presentation spanning desktop and mobile platforms.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : tool.kind === 'prank' && tool.id === 'white-noise-screen' ? (
    <>
      <h2>White noise screen setup and retro TV immersion tips.</h2>
      <p>
        When using the <strong>white noise screen simulator</strong> as a retro TV effect, film background, or display decoration, fullscreen mode is
        the key to the most authentic experience. Press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">F</kbd> or click the
        fullscreen button to hide all browser UI and let the analog static fill the entire display. Audio static starts automatically in fullscreen
        mode, adding the unmistakable sound of a detuned analog television to complete the retro atmosphere.
      </p>
      <p>
        Customize the look and sound through the settings panel (gear icon). Choose from three static styles: Analog for classic gray television snow,
        Color for vibrant rainbow-hued static, or VHS for glitchy drift effects with horizontal banding and sync sway. Toggle the CRT Scanlines overlay
        to simulate the horizontal lines of a vintage cathode-ray tube display. Enable the "NO SIGNAL" indicator badge for an extra layer of television
        authenticity. Adjust the static speed to control how fast the noise pattern updates — faster speeds create a more intense, active static while
        slower speeds produce a calmer, more ambient effect.
      </p>
      <p>
        The audio noise system adds significant immersion. Four audio modes let you choose the right sound: Static (pulsing "seee" sound with breathing
        effect like a detuned analog TV), White (flat consistent hiss ideal for ambience and sound masking), Pink (deeper warmer rumble similar to
        gentle rain), or Off for silent operation. Use the volume slider or press <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">&uarr;</kbd>
        and <kbd class="rounded border border-hairline px-2 py-1 font-mono">&darr;</kbd> to adjust. The white noise screen is ideal for retro TV and VHS
        aesthetic scenes, horror and Halloween displays, cinematic transitions, film production backgrounds, escape room props, streaming intermission
        screens, photography backdrops, gaming room decoration, and creative content production — all without downloading anything.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : tool.kind === 'prank' ? (
    <>
      <h2>Prank setup and performance tips.</h2>
      <p>
        When using {name.toLowerCase()} for a prank, timing and presentation matter as much as the visual effect. Enter fullscreen mode on your device
        before your target sees the screen. If possible, keep the device at a slight angle so the simulated crack lines catch the light naturally.
        The 8 crack patterns cycle through different levels of severity, from subtle spiderweb fractures to dramatic full-shatter effects, so choose
        the one that best matches the situation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The brightness control is especially important for prank realism. Lower brightness works best in dim rooms — a bright cracked screen in a dark
        room can look obviously fake. For daytime office pranks, raise the brightness to match the ambient overhead lighting. The optional timer can
        be set to exit fullscreen automatically after a few seconds as a self-reveal punchline. For the most convincing effect, mute your device
        beforehand — unexpected audio can break the illusion before the visual has time to sink in.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : (
    <>
      <h2>Display testing notes.</h2>
      <p>
        If you are using {name.toLowerCase()} to inspect a device, clean the glass first and check in stable lighting. Dust, fingerprints,
        reflections, and screen protectors can look like panel defects. View the screen straight on, then from shallow angles. Some issues only show
        in one position. For pixel checks, compare multiple solid colors. A pixel that disappears on black but appears on red may be a stuck subpixel
        rather than a completely dead pixel.
      </p>
      <p>
        Browser tools are useful for discovery and documentation, but they are not lab instruments. A color value shown by the page is exact in the
        code, while the emitted light depends on the display, brightness curve, panel technology, operating-system color management, browser behavior,
        and ambient light. For professional color-critical work, pair this utility with calibrated hardware and a color-managed workflow.
      </p>
    </>
  )}
  <h2>Why this page is built differently.</h2>
  <p>
    Many screen utility sites treat every page as a keyword container. PixoraScreen is structured as a product: consistent navigation,
    clear categories, related tools, accessible controls, schema, canonical URLs, hreflang support, and static rendering. The result is faster for
    users and easier for search engines to understand. It also means the page can grow into offline mode, recent colors, favorites, wallpaper
    downloads, timers, and ambient modes without turning into a pile of unrelated widgets.
  </p>
  <p>
    For best results, bookmark the exact page you use most often. Color pages are useful for quick lighting and display checks. Testing pages
    are better when you need a repeatable inspection sequence. Ambient pages are designed for long-running, low-distraction use. Fake and prank
    pages are browser visuals only; they should be used responsibly and never to deceive someone in a harmful context.
  </p>
  {blog && (
    <>
      <h2>{blog.heading}</h2>
      {blog.paragraphs.map((p) => (
        <p set:html={p} />
      ))}
    </>
  )}
  <p>
    {name} is free, accountless, and static-first. That keeps load time low, avoids unnecessary tracking, and makes the tool feel immediate.
    The screen should be available when the user needs it, not after a heavy bundle, a modal, or a signup prompt.
  </p>
</article>
